finding: P1 — src/extras/multiplayer/game.ts:102-107 (also src/extras/multiplayer/bun.ts:64-65): removing a live connection entity or calling runtime.disconnect(eid) drops the peer from the only drained map but never closes/detaches its socket. Reproduced against a real serveGame on loopback, maxClients=1: connect a WebSocket, capture its peer, host.world.destroy(eid), send five binary messages, wait through server ticks. Observed peers=0, entity=false, both client and server socket readyState=1, and peer.connection.receive().length=5. Bun still retains socket.data and its message handler keeps pushing copied messages into an unconsumed queue. A replacement is admitted because peers.size is zero; repeating leaves unlimited live orphan sockets despite maxClients. The connection-entity removal and leave checks pass because they assert only ECS membership/map size, not transport release. The Bun host must observe server-side departure and close/release that transport as well; caller-managed seeded links can remain caller-owned.
